     8	of 2026-08-30 (`collab/review-dao-stream-plan.gpt-5.6-sol.stdout.log`).
     9	
    10	## Strategy
    11	
    12	Two deliberate narrowings:
    13	
    14	1. **A `dao.stream` namespace, not an in-place rewrite.** The legacy
    15	   `dao.stream` has 52 `open!` call sites and every subsystem as a
    16	   consumer; rewriting it in place is an atomic big bang. The new
    17	   namespace is `dao.stream` — one new file,
    18	   `src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc` — building the contract greenfield while
    19	   legacy keeps the system running.
    20	   Consumers migrate one at a time, later, under their own plans. The
    21	   legacy implementation and its tests are evidence about behavior, not
    22	   constraints: no compatibility facade, alias, or dual protocol.
    23	2. **The WebSocket slice, end to end, and nothing else.** One vertical
    24	   slice that forces the contract's riskiest promises early: the
    25	   descriptor round trip across a real serialization boundary, Model A
    26	   serving, writer-only surfaces, deposit admission, and observable
    27	   teardown. Other transports (file, UDP, log, relation, RPC) wait until
    28	   this slice stands.
    29	
    30	The slice still requires a minimal reader-surface core — the served stream
    31	and the deposit destination are both ring buffers — so the ring buffer is
    32	in scope, but only what the contract requires of it, nothing the legacy
    33	one happens to have.
    34	
    35	## Settled before Phase 1
    36	
    37	Three questions a phase would otherwise be forced to invent under delivery
    38	pressure. Each is decided here so no implementer decides it alone.
    39	
    40	**`attach!` is synchronous and never waits.** The Result Convention makes
    41	every operation return an outcome map; the contract's IO Model generalizes
    42	it — no operation waits. No transport in this slice returns a promise, a
    43	future, or a host-native async value from any of the seven operations, on
    44	any host. A ws `attach!` answers what is locally decidable (`ok`,
    45	`invalid-descriptor`) and how the connection resolved arrives as deposited
    46	lifecycle data and through the handle's own `append!` outcomes; see
    47	`dao.stream.ws.md`'s Operations section, corrected to match. A promise
    48	leaking out of a v2 operation is a defect, not a host accommodation.
    49	
    50	The rule covers `create!` equally, though this slice never exercises it:
    51	the ring buffer is in-memory and creates synchronously on every host, and
    52	the ws transport has no `create!` at all — it owns no stream, and starting
    53	an endpoint is a host composition step. A later transport whose creation
    54	needs host resources it cannot acquire synchronously (binding a port,
    55	opening a handle through an async-only API) answers the same way `attach!`
    56	does: the logical stream's identity exists at once, the handle reports
    57	acquisition state through its own operations, and the result of the
    58	acquisition arrives as data. Nothing waits.
    59	
    60	**Deposit admission is declared, never interrogated.** The contract's
    61	public surface has no way to ask a handle its retention policy, and must
    62	not gain one — a predicate answer about a live handle is exactly what
    63	`closed?` was retired for. So the boundary constructor takes the deposit
    64	destination *and its declared retention nature as data*, derived from the
    65	same creation specification that produced the handle, and refuses a
    66	reject-mode declaration at assembly. This is configuration provenance, not
    67	runtime introspection. Runtime behavior is unchanged: every non-`ok`
    68	deposit result tears the connection down.
    69	
    70	**Logical-stream identity is minted in Phase 2.** `cursor-mismatch` in
    71	Phase 2 and stable descriptor identity in Phase 3 are the same identity.
    72	It is allocated when a logical stream is created, and both cursors and
    73	descriptors denote it. Their *representations* need not match and need not
    74	be equally serializable — cursor serialization across hosts stays TBD per
    75	the contract — but they must refer to the same thing.
    76	
    77	## Phase 1 — Contract core (`src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc`)
    78	
    79	The protocol and data surface, with no transport. The seven operations are
    80	two kinds, and the split is the point:
    81	
    82	- **Five handle operations**, as protocols a handle implements per its
    83	  declared surface: `descriptor` (universal identity), `cursor` and `next`
    84	  (reader), `append!` (writer), `close!` (closable).
    85	- **Two transport entry functions**, `create!` and `attach!`, which take no
    86	  handle because none exists yet. These are per-transport functions.
    87	  Dynamic selection is a host-owned map from `:dao.stream/type` to a
    88	  `{create attach}` pair — ordinary data, ordinary lookup, performed by the
    89	  host. DaoStream ships no dispatch machinery: no multimethod, no registry,
    90	  no load-time side effect. Host dispatch is documentation plus an example
    91	  map in tests.
    92	
    93	Putting `create!` or `attach!` on a protocol is how the retired registry
    94	grows back; the file must not do it.
    95	
    96	Also in Phase 1:
    97	
    98	- The result convention: every operation returns an outcome map keyed by
    99	  `:dao.stream/outcome`, open maps, all qualified keywords under
   100	  `:dao.stream/…`, per-operation closed outcome sets exactly as the
   101	  contract's tables state (including the reserved `:dao.stream/denied` and
   102	  `:dao.stream/invalid-value`).
   103	- Generic envelope validation: `:dao.stream/type` present and qualified;
   104	  everything else transport-owned.
   105	- `descriptor` on every handle regardless of surface — identity is
   106	  universal and outlives an attachment, so it must not be bundled into the
   107	  reader or writer protocol.
   108	
   109	Deliverable: a contract-conformance test suite, **surface-aware by
   110	construction**. The harness takes a transport's factories plus its declared
   111	surface set and runs only the applicable blocks: shared laws (result
   112	shapes, envelope validation, descriptor identity, close scope) always;
   113	reader laws (cursor provenance — mint / successor / gap-recovery only —
   114	non-destructive multi-reader reads, anchors) only for handles declaring a
   115	reader surface; writer laws only for writers. A reader suite cannot run
   116	against a WebSocket handle, and a harness that assumes it can will be
   117	discovered in Phase 4 rather than here.
   118	
   119	The suite is *authored* in Phase 1 and first *demonstrated* in Phase 2.
   120	Phase 1 has no transport, so it proves nothing on its own; that is expected
   121	and is not a reason to defer writing it.
   122	
   123	## Phase 2 — Ring buffer reference (`src/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc`)
   124	
   125	The reference reader+writer+closable transport, scoped to the contract:
   126	
   127	- Logical-stream identity minted at `create!`, per *Settled before Phase 1*.
   128	- Evict-oldest retention with declared capacity; `append!` never returns
   129	  `full`; monotonic retention; `gap` with earliest-retained-else-tail
   130	  recovery cursor.
   131	- Cursor anchors `:dao.stream/oldest` and `:dao.stream/newest`;
   132	  `invalid-anchor`; cursors carry the logical-stream identity
   133	  (`cursor-mismatch` / `invalid-cursor` detectable); valid across handles
   134	  of the same logical stream.
   135	- One coherent state per operation (single atom; one deref per `next`),
   136	  satisfying the Concurrency section.
   137	
   138	Explicitly absent: waiters, drain, take, seq views, `closed?`, and **live
   139	resize** — resize is compatible with the contract but proves nothing the
   140	slice needs, and fixed declared capacity is sufficient for deposit
   141	admission. It follows the slice.
   142	
   143	The Phase 1 conformance suite runs here for the first time, with the full
   144	surface set. Ring-buffer-specific tests cover retention, anchors, and
   145	eviction reporting.
   146	
   147	## Phase 3 — Descriptor round trip
   148	
   149	The portable identity story, still in-memory, and constructible without
   150	WebSockets: the contract explicitly permits a host composition to keep a
   151	directory, which is what makes a ring buffer's descriptor resolvable on its
   152	own host.
   153	
   154	- `descriptor` on a ring buffer handle yields a plain-data envelope that
   155	  survives the transit codec structurally unchanged.
   156	- A **host-kept local directory** — a map from stream identity to live
   157	  handle, owned by the test composition, not by DaoStream and not by any
   158	  transport. It is deliberately test-only: the ws serving side in Phase 4
   159	  resolves `:ws/path` against its own served-stream table and does not
   160	  reuse this. Say so in the code, so nobody later mistakes it for
   161	  infrastructure.
   162	- Round-trip test: descriptor → encode → decode → `attach!` against that
   163	  composition → handle on the same logical stream; a kept cursor resumes
   164	  through the new handle. This is where the kept-cursor promise is proven,
   165	  on a transport that has a reader surface.
   166	- `not-found` for descriptors nothing backs.
   167	
   168	**Decision gate — descriptor key set.** The contract leaves the descriptor
   169	envelope TBD and this phase produces the evidence for settling it. Phase 4
   170	must not start against provisional keys: review the round trip's key set,
   171	settle it in `dao.stream.md`, and settle `:ws/…` in `dao.stream.ws.md`
   172	against it. Provisional keys carried into a wire format under delivery
   173	pressure become permanent by accident.
   174	
   175	## Phase 4 — WebSocket transport, forwarding, and serving composition
   176	
   177	Per `dao.stream.ws.md`, both ends. Three deliverables in three layers —
   178	transport, infrastructure, composition — deliberately separate:
   179	
   180	### 4a — The transport (`src/cljc/dao/stream/ws.cljc`)
   181	
   182	- **Client side**: `attach!` connects and returns without waiting (`ok` or
   183	  `invalid-descriptor` only — see *Settled before Phase 1*); the handle is
   184	  writer+closable only; `append!` with transient `full` (including while
   185	  the connection is still establishing), `invalid-value` for unencodable
   186	  values, `closed` once down; `close!` means disconnect, reattachable;
   187	  connection loss is the same detachment, uninvited.
   188	- **Server side**: the accepted-connection handle — writer+closable, minted
   189	  from a socket the runtime handed over, so neither `create!` nor `attach!`
   190	  produces it — with the same `full`, `closed`, `transport-error`,
   191	  send-racing-close, and identity rules as the client handle; plus that
   192	  connection's own boundary adapter for its inbound events.
   193	- **Transport constructor**: the entry functions a host puts in its dispatch
   194	  table are closures this constructor produces, having received the deposit
   195	  destination and its admission declaration. Bare namespace vars could reach
   196	  that state only through namespace globals.
   197	- **Handshake**: encode and recognize the served-stream presentation and the
   198	  authoritative disclaimer specified in `dao.stream.ws.md` — wire protocol, so
   199	  both ends of it are transport, not composition.
   200	- **Boundary adapter**: deposits every inbound event — payload and
   201	  lifecycle, including how the connection resolved, judging nothing — into the
   202	  host-composed deposit destination — one medium per boundary in this slice,
   203	  the granularity being composition policy per `dao.stream.ws.md` — as
   204	  `:dao.stream/attachment`-tagged envelopes, enforcing admission at assembly
   205	  against the declaration passed alongside the destination, and tearing the
   206	  connection down on any non-`ok` deposit result.
   207	- No callbacks in the public surface; the legacy `on-open!`/`on-message!`/
   208	  `on-close!` shape does not reappear.
   209	- **Dependency check**: `v2/ws.cljc` requires only `dao.stream` — never
   210	  the ringbuffer namespace. The ws transport depends on the protocols; the
   211	  ring buffer is the slice's composition partner (deposit destination,
   212	  served stream), wired by the host composition, not by the transport. A
   213	  transport requiring another transport is the legacy registry defect
   214	  reborn.
   215	
   216	### 4b — Forwarding (`src/cljc/dao/stream/forward.cljc`)
   217	
   218	Serving is what makes a stream remotely attachable, and nothing in the
   219	transport transfers a served stream's values to a connected socket. Under
   220	the contract that transfer is an interpreter: it reads one stream through
   221	its own cursor and appends to another. Streams notify no one, so nothing
   222	drives it — it polls, and when `next` answers `blocked` it schedules itself
   223	and asks again.
   224	
   225	That loop is not WebSocket-specific. It reads *any* reader handle and
   226	appends to *any* writer handle: ring buffer to socket, ring buffer to ring
   227	buffer, the code is identical. Its transport-agnosticism is the proof that
   228	it belongs neither inside a transport nor inside one composition. It is a
   229	third layer between them.
   230	
   231	`forward!` owns the three disciplines that are the same everywhere and are
   232	the easiest things in this slice to get wrong:
   233	
   234	- `full` **does not advance the cursor** — nothing was appended, and
   235	  treating the value as forwarded drops it silently;
   236	- `blocked` yields to the scheduler rather than spinning — on a cooperative
   237	  event loop a spin starves the runtime, so the send buffer never drains
   238	  and `full` never clears;
   239	- `end` closes the destination.
   240	
   241	It owns nothing else. The rest are arguments, because each has different
   242	right answers in different compositions:
   243	
   244	- **the scheduler** — "run this again later": `setTimeout` on Node, a
   245	  sleeping thread on the JVM. Cadence is the concern of the runtime driving
   246	  the interpreter, never of the stream (contract, *The readiness
   247	  extension*);
   248	- **the `gap` policy** — resume at the recovery cursor, or tear down. A
   249	  serving pump may skip evicted values; an interpreter that dropped
   250	  submitted work must not. Same outcome, opposite correct responses;
   251	- **the batch budget** — how many values to drain before yielding, which
   252	  exists only because a cooperative event loop needs yielding;
   253	- **starting it.** It never self-installs. The host wires it and starts it
   254	  explicitly, or implicit control flow is back.
   255	
   256	`forward!` is written against the contract rather than against a named
   257	transport, so it is the one interpreter that must be **total over the outcome
   258	algebra** — every outcome of `next` and of `append!`, including those its
   259	particular endpoints never produce. Beyond the disciplines above that means an
   260	explicit branch for destination `closed`, `invalid-value`, and
   261	`transport-error`, and for source `cursor-mismatch` and `invalid-cursor` (its
   262	start cursor is caller-supplied and may not belong to the source). Terminate
   263	and report rather than retry: only `full` and `blocked` are the retryable
   264	answers. Otherwise the contract's totality is bought by moving partiality into
   265	its first consumer.
   266	
   267	Testable with two ring buffers and no socket, so it may be written any time
   268	after Phase 2; only its *use* is Phase 4.
   269	
   270	### 4c — The serving composition (not in `v2/ws.cljc`, not in `forward.cljc`)
   271	
   272	What is left once the loop is infrastructure is the wiring and policy only
   273	the host has. This is a named deliverable, not test wiring, and it must
   274	define:
   275	
   276	- the serving lifecycle: what starts and stops an endpoint, and who owns it;
   277	- the `:ws/path` → served-stream resolution table, host-owned data;
   278	- which identities this endpoint serves — the resolution table's contents.
   279	  The handshake's wire vocabulary is not composition; it belongs to 4a;
   280	- how a connection is associated with the served stream it joined, and how
   281	  that identity reaches deposited events;
   282	- one `forward!` per connection, from the served stream to that
   283	  connection's ws handle, supplied with the scheduler, cadence, batch
   284	  budget, and `gap` policy this composition chooses;
   285	- whether inbound payload events are interpreted into appends on the served
   286	  stream, and if so by which interpreter — the boundary itself judges
   287	  nothing;
   288	- close behavior: what a server-side owner closing the logical stream does
   289	  to connected attachments.
   290	
   291	## Phase 5 — The slice, end to end
   292	
   293	Two processes, not two compositions in one. A same-process socket test is
   294	worth writing earlier for diagnostics, but it cannot show that a descriptor
   295	is self-contained or that no accidental shared state carries identity
   296	across — and those are central contract promises.
   297	
   298	Process A creates a ring buffer, serves it over ws, appends. Process B
   299	receives the descriptor as data, attaches, and appends outbound; B's host
   300	boundary deposits inbound traffic into B's medium; an interpreter in B
   301	reads that medium with cursors. State the bootstrap channel explicitly:
   302	how B obtains A's descriptor is part of the test, not an assumption.
   303	
   304	Kill the connection, then verify four separable facts:
   305	
   306	1. Detachment is observable on both sides — B's handle answers `closed`,
   307	   A's boundary deposits the departure.
   308	2. The served stream survives on A, and `attach!` with the same descriptor
   309	   rejoins the same logical stream.
   310	3. Two simultaneous attachments to the same stream carry distinct
   311	   `:dao.stream/attachment` values, and every event class deposited for one —
   312	   payload, opened, closed, error, resolution — carries its own.
   313	4. B's cursor **on B's deposit medium** still resumes — that medium
   314	   outlives the socket. The cursor is not handed to `attach!` and not used
   315	   through the ws handle, which has no reader surface. WebSocket-level
   316	   resumption — what history a rejoining client receives — is deferred in
   317	   `dao.stream.ws.md` and is not tested here.
   318	
   319	## Host matrix
   320	
   321	- **Phases 1–3** are pure `.cljc` with no transport: they must compile and
   322	  pass on clj, cljs (Node), and cljd. Nothing in them is host-specific, and
   323	  a parity break here is cheapest to find here.
   324	- **Phase 4–5** target clj and cljs (Node) — Node server plus Node client is
   325	  the slice. Browser-client behavior is not validated by a Node target and
   326	  is out of the slice. cljd ws follows once the slice is proven; beware the
   327	  `#?(:cljd nil :clj ...)` reader-conditional trap.
   328	- **`forward.cljc` is host-agnostic** — its only host dependency is the
   329	  scheduler handed to it — so it compiles and its ring-buffer-to-ring-buffer
   330	  tests pass on all three hosts, including where the ws transport does not
   331	  yet exist.
   332	- Per host, name explicitly: how transient send-buffer `full` is detected,
   333	  how a send racing a concurrent close is handled, and that the codec
   334	  produces structurally identical values across all three.
   335	
   336	## Boundary of this plan
   337	
   338	Not in this plan, by design:
   339	
   340	- Migration of any consumer (dao.runtime, yin.vm, dao.jing, dao.space)
   341	  — each gets its own plan against the finished slice.
   342	- Other transports (file, UDP, log, relation, apply/RPC layers).
   343	- The waiter/readiness extension, Shibi, ws resumption protocol, cursor
   344	  serialization across hosts, live ring buffer resize — deferred in the
   345	  contract, deferred here.
   346	
   347	## End condition — v2 is not a permanent fork
   348	
   349	The v2 namespace exists to protect the running system during migration,
   350	not to live forever. The slice is complete when Phases 1–5 pass on clj and
   351	cljs; it is explicitly **incomplete on cljd** until the cljd ws transport
   352	lands, and the end condition is not met before then.
   353	
   354	When the slice is complete and the last consumer has migrated (under its
   355	own plan), legacy `dao.stream` is deleted and a single decision is taken
   356	explicitly: `dao.stream` is renamed to `dao.stream`, or keeps its name
   357	permanently. An undecided coexistence of both namespaces is a defect of the
   358	migration, not a steady state.

 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	# DaoStream WebSocket Transport
     2	
     3	Status: design target, subordinate to `dao.stream.md`. That document is the
     4	contract; this one specifies the WebSocket transport's implementation of it.
     5	Where the two disagree, the contract wins.
     6	
     7	## What the Descriptor Names
     8	
     9	A WebSocket descriptor names a **server-hosted stream**, never a connection.
    10	The stream exists on the serving host — created there, retained there,
    11	closed there by its owner — and it exists whether or not any client is
    12	currently connected. A connection is the mechanics of one attachment:
    13	connecting joins the stream, disconnecting detaches from it, and neither
    14	event creates, closes, or destroys anything. That is what makes `attach!`
    15	honest here: the same descriptor reaches the same stream every time, because
    16	the stream's identity and lifecycle belong to the server, not to the wire.
    17	
    18	This framing generalizes: **serving is what makes any stream remotely
    19	attachable.** A ring buffer is host-local only because nothing speaks for
    20	it. A host that composes a WebSocket endpoint in front of one is serving
    21	that ring buffer; its descriptor now carries an address, and remote
    22	interpreters can attach. The transport owns no stream — it serves streams
    23	the host composed, which is exactly the contract's rule that descriptors
    24	resolve against "the streams a network endpoint serves because serving them
    25	is what it is."
    26	
    27	A server that wants per-conversation state does not bend attachment into
    28	creation: it `create!`s a fresh stream for the conversation and offers that
    29	stream's descriptor.
    30	
    31	## Surfaces
    32	
    33	A WebSocket handle is **writer** and **closable** only. It has no
    34	reader surface: a socket retains nothing, so it cannot make the reader
    35	surface's promise — a positioned, append-only, retained sequence that
    36	cursors can observe and re-observe. `cursor` and `next` do not exist on it.
    37	
    38	## The Duplex Model
    39	
    40	A WebSocket is two independent directions sharing one connection, and the
    41	design keeps them separate:
    42	
    43	- **Outbound**: `append!` on the socket handle encodes the value and
    44	  sends it. Sending is appending; nothing is converted.
    45	- **Inbound**: each arriving message is a host event. The host's adapter
    46	  deposits it — payloads and connection lifecycle events alike, judging
    47	  nothing — onto the stream the **host composition** wired for this
    48	  boundary. Per ADR 0003 there is no separate event bus: dao.space is the
    49	  medium. From that moment the traffic has positions, retention, cursors,
    50	  and gaps — the reading model of the contract applies in full, on that
    51	  stream.
    52	
    53	The deposit destination is wired once, when the host composes the boundary
    54	— it is not a parameter of `attach!`, and no attaching caller chooses or
    55	knows it. Every interpreter takes its own perspective on the same deposited
    56	truth: one indexes the traffic, another copies it to a file, a third ignores
    57	it. How arrivals are interpreted is the interpreter's business, not the
    58	transport's configuration.
    59	
    60	**Attachment identity.** A descriptor names the served stream, so it cannot
    61	distinguish two attachments to it, and a handle is a host-local object that
    62	cannot be compared against data that crossed a codec. So a ws `attach!`
    63	success map carries `:dao.stream/attachment` — a structurally serializable
    64	value, unique per attachment — and every event deposited for that attachment
    65	carries the same value. Without it a shared medium is a multiplexed sequence
    66	with no demultiplexing key: every event present, in order, and
    67	unattributable.
    68	
    69	**Deposited events are envelopes**, so that payload and lifecycle are
    70	distinguishable and both are attributable:
    71	
    72	```clojure
    73	{:dao.stream/attachment <id>
    74	 :ws/event              :ws/payload  ; or :ws/opened :ws/closed :ws/error
    75	                                     ;    :ws/not-found :ws/transport-error
    76	 :ws/value              <decoded>}   ; payload events only
    77	```
    78	
    79	Wrapping is not judging — the adapter never looks inside `:ws/value`. Nothing
    80	of this crosses the wire: the receiving side already knows which socket
    81	delivered a message, so correlation is added locally, on both ends
    82	independently, and the wire carries bare payloads.
    83	
    84	**Granularity is the composition's choice.** One medium per boundary
    85	multiplexes every attachment onto one sequence and one retention window, so a
    86	flood from one attachment evicts another's events. One medium per attachment
    87	isolates them, but the boundary must then announce new attachments on a
    88	boundary-level lifecycle stream, since nothing discovers a stream by itself.
    89	Either shape carries the identity; only the sharing changes.
    90	
    91	```clojure
    92	(def sock (-> (ws/attach! {:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/ws
    93	                           :ws/host "example.org"
    94	                           :ws/port 9090
    95	                           :ws/path "/streams/telemetry"})
    96	              :dao.stream/handle))
    97	
    98	;; write: (append! sock msg)
    99	;; read:  interpreters observe the medium the host boundary deposits into,
   100	;;        filtering on this attachment's identity
   101	```
   102	
   103	Nothing accumulates inside the transport: a hidden per-socket inbox would
   104	be a retention window with an eviction policy nobody composed, sized by
   105	nobody. Retention lives in the medium the boundary deposits into, composed
   106	and sized by the host.
   107	
   108	There are no callback hooks. The legacy `on-open!` / `on-message!` /
   109	`on-close!` surface is retired: every socket event becomes data on the
   110	deposit stream — payload and lifecycle (opened, closed by peer, error)
   111	alike, distinguishable by shape. A boundary deposits everything; whether
   112	anyone reads it is not the boundary's concern.
   113	
   114	### Deposit Admission
   115	
   116	The adapter runs in a callback context: it cannot wait, cannot park, and
   117	cannot record a refused deposit in the medium that refused it. So the
   118	deposit destination must be one that always admits, and this is enforced
   119	where it can be — at assembly, against the destination's declared nature:
   120	
   121	- The destination must declare **evict-oldest retention with a
   122	  host-declared capacity**. It never answers `:dao.stream/full`; under
   123	  pressure, loss surfaces as reported `gap`s at lagging readers — the one
   124	  loss mode the contract blesses. Unbounded in-memory retention is not an
   125	  alternative: it trades a refused deposit for eventual host death, the
   126	  most silent failure of all. (A genuinely durable medium — a disk-backed
   127	  log — plays the same game with a much larger declared window; exhausting
   128	  it at runtime surfaces as `:dao.stream/transport-error` on the deposit,
   129	  like any storage failure.)
   130	- Wiring a reject-mode destination is a **host assembly defect**, refused
   131	  when the boundary is composed — the same category as supplying both
   132	  dispatch shapes. Throwing is reserved for defects detected there, before
   133	  the boundary operates; everything at deposit time is data.
   134	- The destination must outlive the boundary, and **every non-`ok` deposit
   135	  result tears the connection down**: `closed` means the destination is
   136	  gone; `full` or `invalid-value` from a supposedly conforming destination
   137	  is a host or transport defect, evidenced by the returned data;
   138	  `transport-error` is runtime storage failure. In every case, a boundary
   139	  that can no longer turn events into data stops claiming to be attached.
   140	  Disconnection is fully observable — the peer's boundary deposits the
   141	  departure, the local handle answers `:dao.stream/closed`, and
   142	  re-attaching after the host repairs its composition is ordinary
   143	  `attach!`.
   144	
   145	Sizing the window is choosing how much reader lag to tolerate before gaps
   146	appear — an explicit engineering decision in the host's composition. Where
   147	the destination transport supports it, the window may be resized live
   148	(a transport-owned operation on the owning handle): growing it only slows
   149	the eviction horizon, shrinking it is ordinary early eviction reported as
   150	gaps, and positions never renumber. An adaptive policy — grow on rising
   151	gap rate, shrink when quiet — is an interpreter reading its own telemetry,
   152	not a transport behavior.
   153	
   154	The accounting is then complete: overload is a reported `gap`, a dead
   155	destination is an observable disconnection, and silent loss is
   156	unconstructible.
   157	
   158	## Envelope
   159	
   160	Transport-owned keys, qualified under `:ws/…`. The exact key set is TBD
   161	alongside the portable descriptor's definition in the contract; settled
   162	properties:
   163	
   164	- The descriptor is self-contained and exists to cross the network: it
   165	  carries what any host needs to reach the served stream — host and port,
   166	  plus the served stream's identity (a path or stream id; endpoint address
   167	  alone does not name a stream), perhaps TLS parameters.
   168	- The descriptor carries identity only. Where inbound traffic lands is the
   169	  host boundary's composition (see The Duplex Model) and appears in no
   170	  envelope and no argument of `attach!`.
   171	
   172	## The Handshake
   173	
   174	Attaching presents the served stream's identity, and the endpoint either
   175	accepts it or **authoritatively disclaims** it. Both halves are wire protocol
   176	specified here, not per-composition: a client transport must recognize the
   177	disclaimer to deposit `:ws/not-found` rather than a generic failure, so a
   178	composition inventing its own would not interoperate with any client. What a
   179	composition owns is only which identities it serves — its resolution table —
   180	and never the vocabulary of asking and disclaiming.
   181	
   182	## Operations
   183	
   184	- There is no `create!`. The transport owns no stream, so it creates none;
   185	  a host serves a stream it composed elsewhere, and starting an endpoint is
   186	  a host composition step, not a DaoStream operation. A creation
   187	  specification naming this transport returns `:dao.stream/not-found`.
   188	- `attach!` begins connecting and returns without waiting, per the
   189	  contract's rule that no operation waits. It answers what is decidable
   190	  locally, at the moment it is called: `:dao.stream/invalid-descriptor` for
   191	  a descriptor it cannot read, otherwise `:dao.stream/ok` with a handle on
   192	  an attachment still being established. It never returns an answer it has
   193	  not heard. This is not a concession to convenience: on a host with no
   194	  blocking IO the endpoint's answer cannot arrive before `attach!` returns,
   195	  and an `attach!` that waited for it would have to hand back a promise —
   196	  breaking the result convention on three of four hosts and giving the same
   197	  transport different outcome sets per host.
   198	- **How the connection resolved arrives as data**, on the same host-composed
   199	  medium as every other socket event, correlated by attachment identity.
   200	  The distinction survives intact, only its channel moves:
   201	  `:dao.stream/not-found` is the authoritative answer that what the
   202	  descriptor names does not exist — the endpoint responded and disclaimed
   203	  the stream — and is not retried automatically (though a server may serve
   204	  that identity later; retrying on external evidence is interpreter
   205	  policy), while `:dao.stream/transport-error` covers transient
   206	  reachability and connection failures — DNS, timeout, routing, refusal —
   207	  and retrying it may succeed on its own. A caller's retry policy is as
   208	  deterministic as before; it reads the answer from the medium instead of
   209	  from a return value.
   210	- `append!` returns `:dao.stream/full` when the send buffer cannot accept
   211	  the value now — **transient**: the network drains the buffer on its own,
   212	  and a later append succeeds. An attachment still establishing its
   213	  connection answers `full` for the same reason and with the same remedy.
   214	  `:dao.stream/closed` once the connection is down, which includes an
   215	  attachment whose connection never succeeded. A value that cannot be
   216	  encoded for the wire is `:dao.stream/invalid-value`. A local send that
   217	  fails for neither reason is `:dao.stream/transport-error`. Operating the
   218	  handle therefore tells a caller **that** the attachment is not writable,
   219	  needing no reader at all — but never why: an authoritative `not-found` and
   220	  a transient failure both reduce to `closed` here, and the distinction
   221	  survives only as the correlated lifecycle event on the deposit medium.
   222	- `close!` on an attached handle means **disconnect from the server**: the
   223	  handle is on the attachment, so closing it ends the connection,
   224	  idempotently, and this handle's subsequent `append!` returns
   225	  `:dao.stream/closed`. The served stream is untouched — closing *it*
   226	  belongs to its server-side owner. Disconnection is not final: the
   227	  descriptor remains valid and `attach!` rejoins the same stream. The
   228	  peer's boundary deposits the departure into its host-composed event
   229	  medium, correlated by attachment identity.
   230	- Connection loss is the same detachment, uninvited: the served stream
   231	  persists, its descriptor remains valid, and `attach!` with the same
   232	  descriptor rejoins the same stream.
   233	
   234	## Elements and Serialization
   235	
   236	The wire is a serialization boundary. Every element must survive the codec
   237	structurally unchanged; a handle can never travel — its portable
   238	descriptor can.
   239	
   240	## Deferred
   241	
   242	- Exact envelope key set (tracks the contract's descriptor TBD).
   243	- Resumption protocol: stream identity is stable across reconnects, so
   244	  resumption is possible by design; what history a rejoining client receives
   245	  (and how it states where it left off) is not yet specified.
   246	- Backpressure protocols above the stream (a reader publishing its progress
   247	  as data is interpretation, outside both this transport and the contract).
   248	- Relationship to the RPC layers built over streams.

 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	# DaoStream Design
     2	
     3	Status: agreed design target. This document is the DaoStream contract. It does
     4	not redesign Yin.VM. Yin.VM is a downstream consumer and will be addressed only
     5	after this contract is implemented and tested.
     6	
     7	## Purpose
     8	
     9	This contract derives from the foundational axioms and non-negotiable
    10	invariants of [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md). Two axioms bear on it
    11	directly, named here once so the sections below can cite rather than restate
    12	them:
    13	
    14	1. **Everything is a Stream** — all IO and data flow through append-only
    15	   streams.
    16	2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics** — data is syntax; semantics emerge
    17	   only through interpretation. One truth, many perspectives.
    18	
    19	DaoStream is the passive substrate for IO in datom.world — passive because
    20	interpretation and execution must not collapse into one layer. Axiom 1 is
    21	what makes this the layer every host boundary crosses. A stream carries
    22	values and decides nothing about them:
    23	
    24	- **What the values mean** is not its business — Axiom 2. An element
    25	  judged, filtered, or typed at this layer destroys the perspectives nobody
    26	  has taken yet.
    27	- **Which interpreter observes them** is not recorded — a registry of who
    28	  is listening would be hidden global state.
    29	- **When an interpreter runs** is not its decision — "do not introduce
    30	  callbacks; every callback is events on a stream", seen from the stream's
    31	  side: DaoStream invokes nothing, so control is never inverted and
    32	  causality stays explicit (see The IO Model).
    33	
    34	The Invariants below are these denials in operational form.
    35	
    36	The design separates four concepts:
    37	
    38	1. A **creation specification** is data for creating a new logical stream.
    39	2. A **handle** is a host-local operational stream value — the
    40	   implementation of the `dao.stream` protocols.
    41	3. A **portable descriptor** is data used to attach to an existing stream.
    42	4. A **cursor** is immutable interpreter-owned observation state for one
    43	   logical stream.
    44	
    45	Creation is not attachment. A descriptor is not a creation specification. A
    46	handle is not portable identity. A cursor is not handle-owned state.
    47	
    48	Seven operations range over them, each returning an outcome map (see
    49	Result Convention):
    50	
    51	| Operation    | Consumes               | Yields             |
    52	|--------------|------------------------|--------------------|
    53	| `create!`    | creation specification | handle outcome     |
    54	| `attach!`    | portable descriptor    | handle outcome     |
    55	| `descriptor` | handle                 | descriptor outcome |
    56	| `cursor`     | handle, anchor         | cursor outcome     |
    57	| `next`       | handle, cursor         | read outcome       |
    58	| `append!`    | handle, value          | write outcome      |
    59	| `close!`     | handle                 | close outcome      |
    60	
    61	A handle implements the subset of these operations its transport
    62	declares (see Surfaces).
    63	
    64	## Invariants
    65	
    66	- Stream elements are data. DaoStream assigns them no intrinsic meaning.
    67	- Reading is non-destructive. A reader advances its own cursor, not the stream.
    68	- Multiple cursors may observe the same retained value independently.
    69	- Interpreters own cursors and decide when to call `next`.
    70	- A runtime may drive an interpreter, but DaoStream does not require a runtime.
    71	- Closing a stream changes future availability but does not itself erase
    72	  retained history.
    73	- Retention may be bounded. A cursor over evicted history receives a gap
    74	  outcome as data; the reader decides what missing data means.
    75	- All expected operational outcomes are data, not exceptions or callbacks.
    76	- No operation waits. Every operation returns what is true at the moment it is
    77	  called; an answer not yet knowable arrives later as data.
    78	
    79	Every clause below derives from these invariants. A proposed addition that
    80	cannot be derived from one of them does not belong in this contract.
    81	
    82	## Result Convention
    83	
    84	Every DaoStream operation returns data.
    85	
    86	- Each operation's set of outcomes is **exhaustive**: a transport may produce
    87	  fewer of them, never any outside the set. Which subset it produces is part of
    88	  its declared nature (see Surfaces). An outcome is a map whose
    89	  `:dao.stream/outcome` key names the outcome with a qualified keyword. Every
    90	  operation returns such a map — including projections like `descriptor` and
    91	  `cursor`, whose success map carries the projected value.
    92	- Result maps are **open**: the contract fixes which keys are required per
    93	  outcome, and a consumer must ignore keys it does not understand. This is
    94	  what allows future extensions (for example readiness notification) to be
    95	  added without breaking existing consumers.
    96	- Exceptions are reserved for defects in the host's own assembly of
    97	  DaoStream, detected before any operation runs. Everything observable at an
    98	  operation returns data: closed, full, blocked, end, gap, not found,
    99	  malformed input, and transport defects are all outcomes, never exceptions
   100	  and never callbacks.
   101	- All qualified keywords in results live under `:dao.stream/…`. (The legacy
   102	  `:daostream/gap` spelling is retired.)
   103	
   104	## The IO Model
   105	
   106	Three models are available for expressing IO. DaoStream is deliberately the
   107	third.
   108	
   109	**Blocking (synchronous) IO.** The operation parks until it has an answer,
   110	then returns it. Control flow stays sequential and the call site reads as
   111	ordinary code, which is the whole of its appeal. The cost is that something
   112	must wait — a thread per pending operation, held for its duration — and it
   113	requires a host that can park at all.
   114	
   115	**Callback (asynchronous) IO.** The operation registers a continuation and
   116	returns at once; the runtime invokes the continuation when the answer
   117	arrives. Nothing waits, so one thread carries many pending operations: this
   118	is the model that scales. Its cost is inverted control. The caller no longer
   119	decides when it observes the answer, and the inversion is viral — on a host
   120	without blocking, a promise cannot be absorbed inside a function, because
   121	awaiting returns to the caller at the first suspension point and makes the
   122	caller's own result a promise in turn. Whatever is reached from a callback
   123	is reached from a callback.
   124	
   125	**Non-blocking polling.** The operation returns immediately with whatever is
   126	true now, including the answer "nothing yet, ask again." Nothing parks and
   127	nothing is inverted: the caller keeps control and decides when to ask again.
   128	This is POSIX `O_NONBLOCK` without `select`, and it is DaoStream's model.
   129	
   130	`next` is total and non-blocking (see Reading): every state of the stream
   131	has a defined data answer, and `:dao.stream/blocked` is `EAGAIN` — this
   132	position holds nothing yet. `append!`'s `:dao.stream/full` is the same
   133	answer for writes. No operation in this contract parks, and none takes a
   134	callback; the Result Convention's "never exceptions and never callbacks" is
   135	this model stated as a rule about results.
   136	
   137	The rule generalizes past `next`: **no operation waits.** An operation that
   138	cannot yet know its answer returns the outcome true at the moment it is
   139	called, and the eventual answer arrives the way every other fact does — as
   140	data, observed later, through an ordinary operation or on a stream. A
   141	DaoStream operation is never a request for something to happen and be
   142	reported back. It is a question about now.
   143	
   144	### Why this model and not the other two
   145	
   146	The hosts do not offer the same choices. The JVM has both blocking and
   147	asynchronous IO. JavaScript and Dart have only the asynchronous kind: there
   148	is no blocking read to call and no way to wait for one, because the single
   149	event loop that would deliver the answer is the one a waiting call would be
   150	occupying — waiting deadlocks rather than merely costing a thread.
   151	
   152	A blocking contract is therefore unimplementable on most of the targets. A
   153	callback contract is implementable on all of them and inverts control on all
   154	of them, including the host that never needed it. Non-blocking polling is
   155	the only one of the three that means the same thing everywhere — the same
   156	operations, the same outcome sets, the same control flow — which is what
   157	lets one surface serve clj, cljs, and cljd without the shape of the API
   158	differing by host.
   159	
   160	### What it costs
   161	
   162	Polling without readiness notification is O(n) in streams observed: a reader
   163	holding ten thousand idle streams must ask all ten thousand to learn which
   164	have data. This is `select` without `select`, and it is a real limit rather
   165	than an oversight — the extension that answers it is reserved below. The
   166	cost also lands where it can be paid: DaoStream schedules nothing, so
   167	cadence belongs to the runtime driving the interpreters, and a readiness
   168	mechanism belongs there too.
   169	
   170	### The readiness extension
   171	
   172	This contract has no readiness or waiter extension. A reader that receives
   173	`:dao.stream/blocked` retries `next` when it chooses; retry cadence is the
   174	concern of the interpreter or the runtime driving it, not of the stream.
   175	
   176	A future readiness extension would be additive: an optional protocol on
   177	transports that support it, plus optional keys in operation results (for
   178	example a wake token list on `append!`), which the open-map rule already
   179	permits. Nothing in this contract needs to change for that; no consumer of
   180	this contract may depend on it existing.
   181	
   182	## Creation and Attachment
   183	
   184	Creation brings a new logical stream into existence. Attachment joins an
   185	existing one. They are separate operations with separate inputs.
   186	
   187	- `create!` consumes a creation specification and returns a handle on a
   188	  new logical stream. The handle's portable identity is available as a
   189	  descriptor via `descriptor`, whose outcome set is `{ok}` with
   190	  `:dao.stream/descriptor` — identity is universal and outlives any
   191	  attachment, so `descriptor` has no failure outcome.
   192	- `attach!` consumes a portable descriptor and returns a handle on the
   193	  **same** logical stream the descriptor names. Attaching never creates.
   194	- A creation specification says how to build a stream. A descriptor only
   195	  names one — never executable code, never a construction program, never a
   196	  request to load a plugin.
   197	
   198	Streams are values that can be sent through streams — up to a serialization
   199	boundary. An in-memory stream holds references, so a handle can ride it
   200	intact, and an interpreter that receives a handle simply uses it —
   201	every operation takes a handle, and attaching to what you already hold
   202	is meaningless. But a handle does not survive encoding, and any
   203	transport that encodes its elements — a file as much as a network socket —
   204	is a serialization boundary. Descriptors exist for crossing those
   205	boundaries, where the live value cannot travel and a name must. (What a
   206	transport can carry is transport-owned: handing an encoding transport a
   207	value it cannot encode is reported as data like any other outcome.)
   208	`attach!` therefore consumes only descriptors, and
   209	resolves them against the transport's inherent operational state — the
   210	streams a network endpoint serves because serving them is what it is, the
   211	files a filesystem holds. There is no stream table in this contract: no
   212	registry maps descriptors to live handles, so a descriptor arriving
   213	somewhere with nothing behind it — a ring buffer's descriptor on any other
   214	host, or on its own host absent a directory some composition chose to keep —
   215	honestly resolves to `:dao.stream/not-found`.
   216	
   217	### Envelopes
   218	
   219	A **creation specification** is a map with one required key:
   220	
   221	- `:dao.stream/type` — a qualified keyword naming the transport. This is the
   222	  dispatch key.
   223	
   224	Every other key is transport-owned, qualified under the transport's namespace
   225	(for example a ring buffer's capacity), and validated by the selected handler
   226	— generic validation checks only the envelope, and a handler that rejects its
   227	own keys returns `:dao.stream/invalid-spec`.
   228	
   229	The **portable descriptor** is inspired by CORBA's interoperable object
   230	reference. Its exact definition is not designed yet; these properties are
   231	settled, and the key set that realizes them is TBD:
   232	
   233	- It carries `:dao.stream/type` — the transport, as above — as its dispatch
   234	  key.
   235	- It names exactly one logical stream, and that identity is stable: the same
   236	  descriptor attaches to the same stream every time.
   237	- It is self-contained. If the stream is remote, it contains whatever is
   238	  needed to connect — perhaps an IP address and port. The entry data is
   239	  transport-owned; a handler that cannot make sense of it returns
   240	  `:dao.stream/invalid-descriptor`.
   241	- Like an IOR, it may carry authorization: an optional `:dao.stream/shibi`
   242	  key holding Shibi capability tokens that gate whether an interpreter may
   243	  attach. Shibi is not yet designed or built; until it is, the key is
   244	  reserved, no attachment is gated, and the `:dao.stream/denied` outcome
   245	  reserved in the `attach!` table below is unreachable.
   246	
   247	For the time being, the operative guarantee is the round trip: `descriptor`
   248	on a live handle returns `:dao.stream/ok` with `:dao.stream/descriptor`,
   249	a value that can be sent on a stream and reconstructed on the other end,
   250	where `attach!` on the reconstructed value reaches the stream it names —
   251	provided the receiving host can reach what it names at all (see Creation and
   252	Attachment on reachability).
   253	
   254	Both envelopes are plain data through and through: every value must survive
   255	the host serialization codec structurally unchanged, so equality is
   256	structural, and no key holds a function, a host object, a live handle, or
   257	identity smuggled in metadata. Like every DaoStream map, envelopes are open —
   258	a consumer ignores qualified keys it does not understand.
   259	
   260	`create!` outcomes:
   261	
   262	| Outcome                       | Meaning                                       | Required keys        |
   263	|-------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|----------------------|
   264	| `:dao.stream/ok`              | A new logical stream exists.                  | `:dao.stream/handle` |
   265	| `:dao.stream/invalid-spec`    | The creation specification is malformed.      | —                    |
   266	| `:dao.stream/not-found`       | No transport here matches `:dao.stream/type`. | —                    |
   267	| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The handler failed.                           | —                    |
   268	
   269	`attach!` outcomes:
   270	
   271	| Outcome                          | Meaning                                                                             | Required keys        |
   272	|----------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------|
   273	| `:dao.stream/ok`                 | Attached to the existing logical stream.                                            | `:dao.stream/handle` |
   274	| `:dao.stream/invalid-descriptor` | The descriptor is malformed.                                                        | —                    |
   275	| `:dao.stream/not-found`          | The descriptor names no stream here, or no transport matches its type.              | —                    |
   276	| `:dao.stream/denied`             | Attachment refused by capability gating (reserved; unreachable until Shibi exists). | —                    |
   277	| `:dao.stream/transport-error`    | The handler failed.                                                                 | —                    |
   278	
   279	### Host Dispatch
   280	
   281	When a creation specification or portable descriptor arrives dynamically —
   282	for example off a stream — the host looks at it and dispatches on
   283	`:dao.stream/type`. The type resolves to a map that has the transport's
   284	`:dao.stream/create` and `:dao.stream/attach` implementations:
   285	
   286	```clojure
   287	{:dao.stream/ringbuffer {:dao.stream/create ringbuffer/create!
   288	                         :dao.stream/attach ringbuffer/attach!}
   289	 :dao.stream/ws         {:dao.stream/attach ws/attach!}}
   290	```
   291	
   292	The entries are per-transport and may be partial. A transport that creates
   293	no streams supplies no `:dao.stream/create` — the WebSocket transport owns
   294	no stream, it serves streams the host composed, so nothing there creates
   295	one. A creation specification naming it finds no create implementation and
   296	returns `:dao.stream/not-found`, the same answer as an absent transport and
   297	the correct one: nothing here creates that.
   298	
   299	Entry values are **host-composed**: ordinarily closures produced by a
   300	transport constructor that already received whatever composition state the
   301	transport needs — a boundary's deposit destination and its admission
   302	declaration, a directory some composition keeps. The bare vars above are
   303	schematic. A bare namespace var could reach that state only through namespace
   304	globals, which is the hidden state this contract retires.
   305	
   306	This table is ordinary data owned by the host, and the dispatch is an
   307	ordinary map lookup done by the host — DaoStream neither performs nor
   308	standardizes it. A host that cannot dispatch on `:dao.stream/type` returns
   309	`:dao.stream/not-found` — never creation or discovery.
   310	
   311	Code that knows its transport statically calls the transport's functions
   312	directly; no dispatch is involved. There is no ambient namespace-global
   313	registry and no namespace-load registration side effect. (The legacy `open!`
   314	multimethod and `defopen` macro are retired.)
   315	
   316	## Surfaces
   317	
   318	A handle implements the subset of the public surface its transport can
   319	honor — **reader** (`cursor`, `next`), **writer** (`append!`), **closable**
   320	(`close!`) — and the transport declares which. The Cursors, Reading, and
   321	Retention and Gaps sections govern handles with a reader surface only.
   322	
   323	The reader surface is a promise: the handle presents its elements as
   324	one positioned, append-only, retained sequence that cursors can observe and
   325	re-observe. A transport whose medium retains nothing cannot make that
   326	promise and has no reader surface: its inbound events are deposited by the
   327	host's adapter onto the stream the host composition wired for that boundary
   328	— where the reading model applies in full. The log lives beside such a
   329	transport, composed explicitly by the host, never hidden inside the
   330	transport. A deposit destination must admit every valid boundary event per
   331	its declared retention; wiring one that can refuse is a host assembly
   332	defect. (`dao.stream.ws.md` specifies one such transport and the admission
   333	rule in detail.)
   334	
   335	A transport also declares **which outcomes it produces**, and for each one it
   336	excludes, why. There are three honest reasons: the outcome's precondition is
   337	impossible by the transport's nature (an unbounded log never evicts, so never
   338	reports `gap`); another policy answers the same condition, named (a ring buffer
   339	evicts rather than refusing, so pressure surfaces as a reported `gap` and never
   340	as `full`); or the answer is displaced to another channel, named (a transport
   341	that cannot know a remote answer at call time reports it as data elsewhere). An
   342	exclusion with no stated reason is an unimplemented outcome, not a declaration.
   343	
   344	### Where the asynchrony goes
   345	
   346	The IO Model makes this contract non-blocking; hosts are asynchronous
   347	whether or not it is. A network message arrives when the runtime says so, in
   348	a callback, on a thread or event loop DaoStream does not own. DaoStream does
   349	not pretend otherwise; it confines the inversion to one place. The host's
   350	boundary adapter *is* callback code, and its entire job is to `append!` what
   351	arrived. Past that append the event holds a position in a sequence, and
   352	every reader above observes it with its own cursor, in its own control flow,
   353	when it chooses.
   354	
   355	This is an un-inversion. The real cost of callback IO is not performance but
   356	that the inversion spreads through everything it touches; depositing into a
   357	retained sequence pays that cost once, at the boundary, and returns ordinary
   358	code to everyone above. The reader-surface rule above is the same thing
   359	stated structurally: a transport that retains nothing has no reader surface,
   360	and its inbound events are deposited onto a stream that does.
   361	
   362	## Cursors
   363	
   364	A cursor is an immutable value owned by the interpreter that holds it.
   365	
   366	- Cursors are minted by the stream: `cursor` on a handle takes an **anchor**
   367	  and returns `:dao.stream/ok` with `:dao.stream/cursor`. The contract
   368	  defines two anchors — `:dao.stream/oldest`, the earliest retained
   369	  position, and `:dao.stream/newest`, positioned after the newest appended
   370	  value so the first `next` observes the next value to arrive. A transport
   371	  whose medium is naturally addressable may accept additional
   372	  transport-owned anchors (a byte offset, a timestamp). An anchor that is
   373	  malformed or not honored by this transport yields
   374	  `:dao.stream/invalid-anchor`. On a handle whose attachment has closed,
   375	  `cursor` yields `:dao.stream/closed`; a handle on the logical stream
   376	  itself keeps minting cursors after close, so retained history remains
   377	  readable. `cursor`'s outcome set is therefore `:dao.stream/ok` (with
   378	  `:dao.stream/cursor`), `:dao.stream/invalid-anchor`, and
   379	  `:dao.stream/closed`.
   380	- Every valid cursor comes from the stream: minted by `cursor`, received as
   381	  a successor from `next`, or recovered from a gap outcome. Consumers never
   382	  construct cursor internals or fabricate positions — there is no `seek`. A
   383	  cursor's representation belongs to the transport.
   384	- A cursor binds to the logical stream, not to a handle: any handle on the
   385	  same logical stream accepts it, so a cursor handed to another interpreter
   386	  works with that interpreter's own handle. Whether a cursor survives
   387	  serialization to another host is transport-owned and TBD, like the
   388	  descriptor envelope.
   389	- A cursor carries enough identity to detect misuse — the mismatch outcomes
   390	  in the Reading table depend on it.
   391	- Advancing is receiving a successor cursor from `next`. The stream never
   392	  advances a reader; the stream holds no reader positions at all. Dropping a
   393	  cursor is how a reader detaches — there is nothing to unregister.
   394	- Because cursors are immutable values, a reader may retain an old cursor and
   395	  re-read retained history, or hand a cursor to another interpreter. What a
   396	  cursor can still observe is governed only by retention.
   397	
   398	## Reading
   399	
   400	`next` takes a handle and a cursor and returns an outcome immediately.
   401	It is total and non-blocking: every state of the stream has a defined data
   402	answer.
   403	
   404	| Outcome                       | Meaning                                                                       | Required keys                                                                           |
   405	|-------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
   406	| `:dao.stream/ok`              | A value was observed.                                                         | `:dao.stream/value`, `:dao.stream/cursor` (successor)                                   |
   407	| `:dao.stream/blocked`         | Nothing at this position yet; retry later.                                    | —                                                                                       |
   408	| `:dao.stream/end`             | What this handle is on is closed and this position is past the retained tail. | —                                                                                       |
   409	| `:dao.stream/gap`             | This position was evicted.                                                    | `:dao.stream/cursor` (earliest retained position, or the tail when nothing is retained) |
   410	| `:dao.stream/cursor-mismatch` | The cursor was minted by a different logical stream.                          | —                                                                                       |
   411	| `:dao.stream/invalid-cursor`  | The value is not a cursor.                                                    | —                                                                                       |
   412	
   413	Reading never mutates the stream. Two readers with cursors at the same
   414	position observe the same retained value; neither can starve or affect the
   415	other. There is no destructive read anywhere in the contract — no drain, no
   416	take. An operation that removes an element as it reads would make one
   417	reader's progress every other reader's data loss.
   418	
   419	## Writing
   420	
   421	`append!` takes a handle and a value and returns an outcome:
   422	
   423	| Outcome                       | Meaning                                                                                                                                              | Required keys |
   424	|-------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------|
   425	| `:dao.stream/ok`              | The value is accepted at the next position in the sequence this handle's writer surface is on. Implies neither local readability nor remote delivery. | —             |
   426	| `:dao.stream/full`            | The transport cannot accept the value now; nothing was appended.                                                                                     | —             |
   427	| `:dao.stream/invalid-value`   | The transport cannot carry this value (for example, it cannot be encoded); nothing was appended.                                                     | —             |
   428	| `:dao.stream/closed`          | What this handle is on is closed to new appends; the value was not appended.                                                                         | —             |
   429	| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The transport failed to perform the append; nothing was appended.                                                                                    | —             |
   430	
   431	Acceptance is handle-relative, as close outcomes are. **Which sequence** a
   432	handle's writer surface is on is part of the transport's declared nature: for a
   433	ring buffer it is the logical stream itself, so `ok` places the value in it; for
   434	a transport that carries values toward a stream elsewhere it is the ordered
   435	outbound path, and what becomes of the value at the far end is reported there,
   436	not here. No remote transport can say more — under *no operation waits*, an
   437	answer from the far end cannot arrive before `append!` returns.
   438	
   439	The writer decides what to do about any non-`ok` outcome; DaoStream does not
   440	retry, buffer, or notify on its behalf.
   441	
   442	## Close
   443	
   444	`close!` takes a handle and returns a map whose outcome set is `{ok}` — no
   445	other outcome exists for it. It is idempotent: closing an already-closed scope
   446	is also `:dao.stream/ok`.
   447	
   448	`close!` closes **what the handle is on**. A handle that owns a stream — its
   449	creator, or its server-side host — closes the logical stream. An attached
   450	handle is on an attachment: closing it ends that attachment only — the
   451	stream persists, its descriptor remains valid, and `attach!` may rejoin
   452	later. Which scope a transport's handles carry is part of its declared
   453	nature.
   454	
   455	Outcome meanings are handle-relative throughout: `:dao.stream/closed` and
   456	`:dao.stream/end` speak of what *this handle* is on. After an attachment
   457	closes, the handle's `append!` answers `closed`; `descriptor` still answers
   458	`ok` — identity outlives any attachment; a reader surface, where the
   459	handle has one, answers `end` from `next` once observation through this
   460	handle is exhausted, and `closed` from `cursor` — no new cursors are
   461	minted through a closed attachment. Cursors bind to the logical stream,
   462	not to a handle, so a kept cursor resumes through a newly attached handle.
   463	
   464	Closing the logical stream changes future availability only:
   465	
   466	- Subsequent `append!` returns `:dao.stream/closed`.
   467	- A reader that exhausts retained history receives `:dao.stream/end` instead
   468	  of `:dao.stream/blocked`.
   469	- Retained history remains readable. Close erases nothing; eviction is a
   470	  retention concern, not a lifecycle one.
   471	
   472	There is no `closed?` predicate in the public surface. Operation results are
   473	authoritative; a predicate answer is stale the moment it is returned.
   474	
   475	## Concurrency
   476	
   477	Each logical stream is one append-only sequence: a successfully appended
   478	value occupies exactly one definite position in that sequence, and every
   479	operation's outcome reflects the sequence at one moment. DaoStream
   480	guarantees nothing further about concurrent operations.
   481	
   482	This follows from Axiom 2. The sequence is the one truth; each outcome is
   483	one perspective on it at one moment. A reader that observes
   484	`:dao.stream/blocked` and then a value has not caught the stream in a
   485	contradiction — it made two observations from its own cursor, and no global
   486	view exists to contradict. Whether an append landed before a close is
   487	answered by the sequence itself: the value has a position or it does not.
   488	What an ordering between writers *means*, and any coordination among them —
   489	a merge interpreter, a one-log-per-writer discipline — is interpretation,
   490	and interpretation happens above the stream.
   491	
   492	## Retention and Gaps
   493	
   494	Retention and overflow behavior are transport-owned, declared by the
   495	creation specification. A ring buffer retains a bounded window and evicts
   496	the oldest; a file or append-only log retains everything; a network send
   497	buffer refuses transiently. Which outcomes a given stream can produce —
   498	`full` on append, `gap` on read — and whether its `full` is transient or
   499	permanent follow from the transport's declared nature. DaoStream names the
   500	outcomes and standardizes no policy.
   501	
   502	When bounded retention evicts a value, a cursor still pointing at it gets the
   503	gap outcome from the Reading table. The stream reports honestly and decides
   504	nothing: an interpreter replaying a log may treat a gap as fatal; a live
   505	telemetry viewer may adopt the returned cursor and continue. Silently
   506	skipping evicted history is forbidden — a reader must be able to know it
   507	missed values.
   508	
   509	## Composition
   510	
   511	DaoStream defines only streams, cursors, and the operations above. Anything
   512	that transforms one stream into another is an interpreter — it reads via its
   513	own cursor and appends to an ordinary output stream — and that lives entirely
   514	outside this contract, needing no support from it. As in a Unix pipeline, the
   515	pipe is dumb; the transforming happens in the processes on either end.
   516	
   517	## Explicitly Absent
   518	
   519	Absent from the public surface, by derivation from the invariants: `open!`
   520	and `defopen` (ambient registry), `closed?` (stale predicate), `drain-one!`
   521	and any destructive read (readers advance cursors, not the stream), `seek`
   522	(every valid cursor comes from the stream; anchored minting and kept
   523	cursors cover repositioning), `take!!` (blocking), waiter registration
   524	(no readiness extension), and throwing conveniences such as `strict-vec`
   525	(operational outcomes are data).

