Verdict: request changes. No critical/blocker findings, but five high-severity contradictions remain.

## High

- HIGH | [dao.stream.ws.md:188](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:188) | `attach!` returns `ok` before it knows whether the named stream exists. The contract defines `ok` as “attached to the existing logical stream” ([dao.stream.md:273](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:273)) and requires every result to describe what is true now ([dao.stream.md:76](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:76)). A later `:ws/not-found` proves the earlier `ok` was false. Outcome displacement permits postponing the remote answer; it does not permit substituting a false success. | Add an exhaustive `attach!` outcome such as `:dao.stream/pending`, carrying the provisional handle and attachment identity. Reserve `ok` for transports that can establish attachment synchronously. Alternatively weaken the contract’s meaning of attachment and `ok`, but that is a broader and less desirable relaxation.

- HIGH | [dao.stream.md:425](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:425) | The handle-relative `append!` relaxation is necessary but not fully integrated. The contract still says every successfully appended value occupies a position in the logical stream ([dao.stream.md:477](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:477)), while ws may never append inbound traffic to the served stream ([dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:285](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:285)). Moreover, the ws spec never makes the required declaration identifying its writer sequence; “encodes and sends” is not a definition of that sequence or its ordering guarantee ([dao.stream.ws.md:43](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:43)). | Restrict the Concurrency claim to appends whose writer surface is the logical stream. Require every writer transport to identify its acceptance order and its relationship, if any, to the descriptor-named stream. For ws, say explicitly that `ok` records local outbound acceptance only, preserves order among values that reach the peer, and promises neither peer deposit nor served-stream insertion. Add conformance tests for this weaker promise. This is approximately the minimal viable relaxation, but the current text is not yet sound because it leaves the older logical-stream claim in force.

- HIGH | [dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:222](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:222) | `forward!` “schedules itself” through an injected scheduler, with `setTimeout` as the concrete example ([dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:244](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:244)). That is callback-driven implicit control flow, contrary to “every callback is events on a stream” and the contract’s statement that cadence belongs to the runtime driving interpreters ([dao.stream.md:166](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:166)). | Specify `forward!` as a total, single-step state transition. It should return or emit explicit next state—progress, retryable blocked/full, or terminal report—and let the runtime decide when to invoke the next step. Any host timer completion must first become an event on a stream.

- HIGH | [dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:30](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:30) | The plan makes a ring buffer the production deposit destination, while its superior ws specification states that `dao.space` is the medium ([dao.stream.ws.md:48](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:48)). The accepted ADR is equally explicit that host boundaries deposit into `dao.space` ([0003-dao-space-is-the-event-medium.md:26](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/adr/0003-dao-space-is-the-event-medium.md:26)). Calling the ring buffer “one medium per boundary” does not cure the substitution. | Use a conforming `dao.space` writer as the Phase 4–5 deposit destination. Ring buffers can remain isolated transport and forwarding fixtures. If the ring buffer is deliberately a slice-only stand-in, the ws document and ADR must first grant that exception explicitly.

- HIGH | [dao.stream.ws.md:69](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:69) | Making deposited events a mandatory bespoke envelope and closed transport event taxonomy conflicts textually with the governing Host Boundaries rule that host events need no bespoke envelope or fact taxonomy ([datom.world.md:101](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/datom.world.md:101)). Attachment correlation is necessary, but the correction widened the ws public data surface without reconciling that authority. | Either amend the governing rule to permit minimal transport-owned ordinary event maps where correlation/classification requires them, or remove the mandated wrapper and specify correlation as fields on ordinary transformed event values. The subordinate ws document cannot resolve this conflict by itself.

## Medium

- MEDIUM | [dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:239](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:239) | A generic forwarder from “any reader” to “any writer” cannot universally close the destination on source `end`. Writer and closable are independent surfaces ([dao.stream.md:318](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:318)); even when closable, closing an owning handle can close a logical stream shared with unrelated writers. | Make end handling composition policy, defaulting to terminate-and-report without closing. Alternatively require a writer+closable destination whose ownership is exclusive, which substantially narrows the generic claim.

- MEDIUM | [dao.stream.ws.md:172](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:172) | The new Handshake section assigns ownership but supplies no actual wire vocabulary: no frame/status/message shape defines presentation or authoritative disclaimer. Separately, the event envelope names `:ws/not-found` and `:ws/transport-error` ([dao.stream.ws.md:74](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:74)), while Operations describes deposited resolution values as `:dao.stream/not-found` and `:dao.stream/transport-error` ([dao.stream.ws.md:201](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:201)). Independent implementations cannot interoperate or write a precise oracle. | After the descriptor-key decision gate, specify the actual handshake representation and one canonical deposited-event discriminator vocabulary. Distinguish `:ws/error` from `:ws/transport-error`, or remove one.

- MEDIUM | [dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:109](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:109) | The contract now requires each transport to declare its produced outcome subset and justify every exclusion ([dao.stream.md:335](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:335)), but the planned harness receives only factories and surface sets. Phase 2 also states selected ring-buffer exclusions—principally `full`—without declaring the complete per-operation profiles or explaining exclusions such as `invalid-value`, `transport-error`, and attachment-specific `cursor/closed`. | Add an outcome profile to every transport declaration and to the conformance harness. For alternative-policy/displaced exclusions, require a driver, observation point, oracle, and correlation assertion; for structurally impossible outcomes, record the invariant and representative boundary tests.

- MEDIUM | [dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:112](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:112) | The harness runs “close scope” laws always, although closable is an optional surface. That makes the supposedly surface-aware suite invalid for a reader-only or writer-only, non-closable handle. | Move close laws into a closable-only block. Keep descriptor identity and generic result-shape laws universal.

## Low

No low-severity findings.

The additions of `append!` transport error, explicit `descriptor`/`cursor`/`close!` outcome sets, host-composed dispatch entries, ws attachment identity, a named server-side handle, and explicit `forward!` outcome branches are individually correct. The exhaustive-upper-bound/subset model is also sound. Its main remaining defect is that displaced failure was used to justify a premature `attach!` success, which the model does not authorize.
